n Christian College","University of the Punjab","Sadiq Public School"],"occupations":["banker","politician"],"wiki":"Muhammad Mian Soomro"},"Q57373":{"id":"Q57373","name":"Asif Ali Zardari","desc":"President of Pakistan (2008–2013; since 2024)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Asif_Ali_Zardari_-_2024_(cropped_2).jpg/330px-Asif_Ali_Zardari_-_2024_(cropped_2).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1955-07-26","died":null,"birthplace":"Karachi","parties":["Pakistan Peoples Party"],"education":["St Patrick's High School, Karachi","Cadet College Petaro"],"occupations":["politician","entrepreneur"],"wiki":"Asif Ali Zardari"},"Q57811":{"id":"Q57811","name":"Raja Pervez Ashraf","desc":"Pakistani politician","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Raja_Pervez_Ashraf.jpg/330px-Raja_Pervez_Ashraf.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1950-12-26","died":null,"birthplace":"Sanghar","parties":["Pakistan Peoples Party"],"education":["University of Sindh"],"occupations":["politician","entrepreneur"],"wiki":"Raja Pervez Ashraf"},"Q14257286":{"id":"Q14257286","name":"Mamnoon Hussain","desc":"12th president of Pakistan from 2013 to 2018","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/President_Mamnoon_Hussain.jpg/330px-President_Mamnoon_Hussain.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1940-12-23","died":"2021-07-14","birthplace":"Agra","parties":["Pakistan Muslim League (N)"],"education":["Institute of Business Administration, Karachi","University of Karachi"],"occupations":["politician","entrepreneur"],"wiki":"Mamnoon Hussain"},"Q7461686":{"id":"Q7461686","name":"Shahid Khaqan Abbasi","desc":"Pakistani politician and businessman","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Shahid_Khaqan_Abbasi_(cropped).jpg/330px-Shahid_Khaqan_Abbasi_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1958-12-27","died":null,"birthplace":"Karachi","parties":["Pakistan Muslim League (N)"],"education":["George Washington University","University of California, Los Angeles","Lawrence College Ghora Gali","Habib Public School"],"occupations":["politician","engineer","businessperson"],"wiki":"Shahid Khaqan Abbasi"},"Q155164":{"id":"Q155164","name":"Imran Khan","desc":"Prime Minister of Pakistan from 2018 to 2022","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Prime_Minister_Imran_Khan_Adresses_the_Forum_01.jpg/330px-Prime_Minister_Imran_Khan_Adresses_the_Forum_01.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1952-10-05","died":null,"birthplace":"Lahore","parties":["Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf"],"education":["Keble College","Aitchison College","Royal Grammar School Worcester","University of Oxford"],"occupations":["politician","cricketer","autobiographer","investor"],"wiki":"Imran Khan"},"Q4790529":{"id":"Q4790529","name":"Arif Alvi","desc":"13th President of Pakistan from 2018 to 2024","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/President_of_Pakistan_Dr_Arif_Alvi_(cropped).jpg/330px-President_of_Pakistan_Dr_Arif_Alvi_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1949-07-29","died":null,"birthplace":"Karachi","parties":["Jamaat-e-Islami","independent politician","Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf"],"education":["De'Montmorency College of Dentistry","University of Michigan","University of the Pacific"],"occupations":["dentist","politician"],"wiki":"Arif Alvi"},"Q2096387":{"id":"Q2096387","name":"Shehbaz Sharif","desc":"Prime Minister of Pakistan (2022–2023; 2024–present)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shehbaz_Sharif_in_2024.jpg/330px-Shehbaz_Sharif_in_2024.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1951-09-23","died":null,"birthplace":"Lahore","parties":["Pakistan Muslim League (N)"],"education":["St. Anthony's High School","Government College University"],"occupations":["politician"],"wiki":"Shehbaz Sharif"}},"summary":"Pakistan, officially the Islamic Republic of Pakistan, is a country in South Asia. It is the fifth-most populous country, with a population of over 241.5 million, having the second-largest Muslim population in the world as of 2023. Islamabad is the nation's capital, while Karachi is its largest city and financial centre. Pakistan is the 33rd-largest country by area. Bounded by the Arabian Sea on the south, the Gulf of Oman on the southwest, and the Sir Creek on the southeast, it shares land borders with India to the east; Afghanistan to the west; Iran to the southwest; and China to the northeast. It shares a maritime border with Oman in the Gulf of Oman, and is separated from Tajikistan in the northwest by Afghanistan's narrow Wakhan Corridor.\nPakistan is the site of several ancient cultures, including the 8,500-year-old Neolithic site of Mehrgarh in Balochistan, the Indus Valley Civilisation of the Bronze Age, and the ancient Gandhara civilisation. The regions that compose the modern state of Pakistan were the realm of multiple empires and dynasties, including the Achaemenid, the Maurya, the Kushan, the Gupta; the Umayyad Caliphate in its southern regions, the Hindu Shahis, the Ghaznavids, the Delhi Sultanate, the Samma, the Shah Miris, the Mughals, and finally, the British Raj from 1858 to 1947.\nSpurred by the Pakistan Movement, which sought a homeland for the Muslims of British India, and election victories in 1946 by the All-India Muslim League, Pakistan gained independence in 1947 after the partition of British India, which awarded separate statehood to its Muslim-majority regions and was accompanied by an unparalleled mass migration and loss of life. Initially a dominion of the British Commonwealth, Pakistan adopted a republican constitution in 1956 and became an Islamic republic with two geographically separate provinces, East Pakistan and West Pakistan. East Pakistan seceded as the new country of Bangladesh in 1971 after a nine-month-long civil war. In the following four decades, Pakistan has been ruled by governments that alternated between civilian and military, democratic and authoritarian, relatively secular and Islamist.\nPakistan is considered a middle power and is a declared nuclear-weapon state, with the world's seventh-largest standing armed forces. The Pakistani economy is ranked amongst the world's emerging and growth-leading economies, with a large and rapidly growing middle class. Pakistan's political history since independence has been characterised by periods of significant economic and military growth as well as those of political and economic instability. It is an ethnically and linguistically diverse country, with similarly diverse geography and wildlife. The country continues to face poverty, illiteracy, corruption, and terrorism. Pakistan is a member of the United Nations, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, the Commonwealth of Nations, the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation, and the Islamic Military Counter-Terrorism Coalition, and is designated as a major non-NATO ally by the United States.","history":"The history of Pakistan preceding the country's independence in 1947 is shared with that of neighbouring Afghanistan, India, Iran and Tibet. Spanning the western expanse of the Indian subcontinent and the eastern borderlands of the Iranian plateau, the region of contemporary Pakistan served both as the fertile ground of a major civilization and as the gateway of South Asia to Central Asia and the Near East.\nSituated on the first coastal migration route of Homo sapiens out of Africa, the region was inhabited early by modern humans. The 9,000-year history of village life in South Asia traces back to the Neolithic (7000–4300 BCE) site of Mehrgarh in Pakistan,  and the 5,000-year history of urban life in South Asia to the various sites of the Indus Valley Civilisation, including Mohenjo-daro and Harappa.\nThe ensuing millennia saw the region of present-day Pakistan absorb diverse influences. Several ancient empires left their mark in the region, such as the Achaemenid, the Maurya, the Indo-Greek, the Indo-Scythian, the Indo-Parthian, the Kushan, the Apracha and the Odi in Gandhara, the Huna and the Takka; as did, after the Islamic conquest, the Umayyad, the Ghaznavid, the Ghurid, the Delhi Sultanate, and the Mughal, before the modern period. During the medieval period, dynasties originating from the modern-day country ruled various areas in and around Pakistan, including the Patola, the Odi, the Soomra, the Samma, the Qambrani, the Langah, the Sayyid and the Lodi of Delhi Sultanate, the Gibari, the Shah Miri, the Maqpon, the Kalhora, the Talpur, the Gichki, the Sukerchakia, and the Daudpotra, among others.\nGradually, in the first half of the 19th century, the region came under suzerainty of the East India Company through conquests and subsidiary alliances. Company Raj was replaced by the direct crown rule, the British Raj, after 1858. In the early decades of the 20th-century, Muslim nationalism gained momentum in the predominantly Muslim population and intelligentsia, and the Pakistan Movement, led by Muhammad Ali Jinnah, ended with the partition of British holdings in 1947 and the establishment of the dominions of Pakistan and India. The heritage of the country is represented among others in the ancient, mainly Hindu-Buddhist, sites of Taxila, and Takht-i-Bahi, the 14th-century Islamic-Sindhi monuments of Thatta, and the 17th-century Mughal monuments of Lahore. \n\n","historyTitle":"History of Pakistan","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,45709300],[1961,46921300],[1962,48156100],[1963,49447800],[1964,50800000],[1965,52209000],[1966,53678700],[1967,55214800],[1968,56810500],[1969,58460900],[1970,60166700],[1971,61847900],[1972,63583200],[1973,65461900],[1974,67415500],[1975,69461200],[1976,71620300],[1977,73901800],[1978,76307400],[1979,78997000],[1980,82291000],[1981,85802000],[1982,89089200],[1983,92227700],[1984,95164900],[1985,98229300],[1986,101607000],[1987,105131000],[1988,108725000],[1989,112337000],[1990,116156000],[1991,120060000],[1992,123547000],[1993,127020000],[1994,130